From eb8daa247e063510d14cd5e472c8dfb6016ae99d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Virtroid build <build@virtroid.invalid>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 13:09:12 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] codec2: add c2.virtroid.x264.encoder, the screen encoder

With no hardware encoder, a software H.264 encoder streams the screen.
On frames recorded from the runtime's own gestures (drawer, shade,
Settings scrolling, recents) and encoded on one core at the same
settings, libavc at its fastest preset needs about 3.1 Mbps for 50 dB
PSNR at 4.0 ms a frame, and its worst frames fall to about 30 dB; x264
at its superfast preset needs about 1.7 Mbps at 5.5 ms a frame, with
worst frames about 45 dB.

The new component wraps x264 behind the same Codec2 interface as
c2.android.avc.encoder:
 - superfast, tuned for zero latency: no lookahead, no B-frames, each
   frame out as it goes in, on the component's thread (x264 is built
   without threads of its own), raised to ANDROID_PRIORITY_URGENT_DISPLAY
   so it never waits behind the apps' render threads;
 - rate control by the configured frame rate, not the frames'
   timestamps (with timestamps x264 holds each frame until the next one
   arrives, and the screen sends one only when it changes), with the
   bitrate held within a second (half for CBR), and live bitrate changes
   and key frame requests honoured;
 - no IDR frames but the periodic and requested ones; SPS and PPS once,
   as codec-specific data; High profile unless asked otherwise.

It is ranked 256, ahead of the software default 0x200, so video/avc
encodes with it. x264 (GPL-2.0-or-later) is libx264_virtroid in
device/virtroid/x264, which deploy/aosp/build-x264.sh builds with the
NDK; the library that links it is distributed under the GPL.

